---
source: crates/tungstate-cli/tests/cli.rs
expression: help
---
Record a place link ends can live, other than this machine

Usage: tungstate connection add [OPTIONS] --scheme <SCHEME> <NAME>

Arguments:
  <NAME>
          What this connection is called on the command line

Options:
      --scheme <SCHEME>
          Which protocol it speaks: fs (a folder this machine can reach), smb, sftp, webdav, ftps, ftp or s3

      --host <HOST>
          Hostname, for the schemes that have one

      --port <PORT>
          Port, where it differs from the protocol default

      --user <USERNAME>
          Who to connect as. For S3, the access key id

      --root <ROOT>
          Absolute path on the far side that every link path is relative to.
          
          For a remote this is a path on the server, not on this machine, and it is often not the same as the directory you land in when you log in: many servers put you in `/home/you` while `/` is the whole disk. For SMB it starts with the share; for S3 it is a folder inside the bucket. `connection test` prints what it found there, so check it.
          
          [default: ""]

      --bucket <BUCKET>
          S3: the bucket every path lives in

      --region <REGION>
          S3: the bucket's region. Needed for AWS unless the bucket is in us-east-1; other services are asked, and most accept any

      --endpoint <ENDPOINT>
          S3: the service's address, for anything that is not AWS itself, as in `https://s3.eu-central-003.backblazeb2.com` or `http://nas.local:9000`. WebDAV: the server's address, required, as in `https://nas.local:5006`

      --share <SHARE>
          SMB: the share, which the root then continues inside. `--share media --root backups` is the same as `--root media/backups`

      --key <PATH>
          SFTP: sign in with this private key file instead of a password. The secret asked for is then the key's passphrase, if it has one

      --option <KEY=VALUE>
          Per-scheme extra, as `key=value`. Repeatable. SMB reads `encryption=required`, to refuse a server that will not encrypt

      --secret-stdin
          Read the password, or S3 secret key, from stdin rather than prompting, for scripts

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')
